WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Robert Rippberger, Filmmaker: This person&nbsp;
could get upset about something and could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.080
walk and the entire entire day would shut down.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:12.160
Film Courage: Does a director automatically&nbsp;
deserve respect once they show up on set?

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:18.320
Robert: A director doesn't deserve respect but a&nbsp;
director better earn people's respect or it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:26.560
going to be a very good set. For me there's some&nbsp;
core things, one is that I am always the hardest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.920
working on set. If other people are working longer&nbsp;
I'll be there with them. There's been times where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:40.160
I've helped load G/E trucks, there's been times&nbsp;
where I've helped mix blood and mud and all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:51.440
things and everybody knows that I value their time&nbsp;
extremely and their contribution as fellow artists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:04.800
and it's super important to me that I respect them&nbsp;
and that it's vice versa. To use the Hannah Arendt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.040
line that it's action in concert and it's so much&nbsp;
about the sum of the parts which also goes to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.040
if people aren't respectful then we get them off&nbsp;
set. That it really is your people who want to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.040
there, your people who are engaged, your people&nbsp;
who have bright ideas and your people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:29.920
in a frame of mind to really produce as good of&nbsp;
possible film as possible what's your image of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:37.360
bad director I think an image of a bad director&nbsp;
is one that doesn't listen to outside ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:50.880
that feels like they're better than other people&nbsp;
that is also just not a very nice person I really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.480
feel film is such a collaborative medium and&nbsp;
that's where you are getting your best ideas and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:04.560
some of the the directors that I look up to have&nbsp;
always treat create have always treated their sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:14.720
as as almost families and so yeah I think if you&nbsp;
don't respect those that are doing the work then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.800
then you're you shouldn't be a director it sounds&nbsp;
like it do going back to what we talked about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:27.200
one philosopher not not just their philosophy but&nbsp;
their whole sort of idea of being not outside of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:33.520
the world but in the world yeah sorry who was&nbsp;
that Martin Heidegger Knight here okay yeah and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:39.760
and that sounds like you said you like to operate&nbsp;
like that in terms of you're you're in the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:46.000
you're in the set that you're they're all these&nbsp;
moving pieces yeah exactly it's it's sort of like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.480
acceptance of like the infinite vulnerability of&nbsp;
being on a set and for me it's leaning into that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:03:00.000
even further and knowing like this person could&nbsp;
get upset about something and could walk and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.720
entire entire day would shut down right like that&nbsp;
that everybody is treated with the value of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:16.080
enormous like pot of gold that they hold which&nbsp;
is to to keep the film going and and to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:24.080
everything everything elevated and it's also just&nbsp;
it's also for me it's just common decency it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.080
like people are there they're working and they're&nbsp;
putting a lot out they're sacrificing not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:37.200
with family and doing other things and and yeah&nbsp;
just showing how grateful and and appreciative you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:45.120
are of that is is is really important to me can we&nbsp;
talk about the situation where sometimes we will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.920
be around energy that won't be as collaborative&nbsp;
and it may or may not be anything that we've done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:56.480
things happen people have bad days people&nbsp;
have agendas how do you if you how do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.840
write the the ship if you feel it's getting&nbsp;
away from you the super important thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:09.040
about directing is very clear communication and&nbsp;
if there's problems addressing them immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:18.480
so there's been a lot of times where people said&nbsp;
kind of under their breath comments and I'll stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:22.560
them I'll take them to the side I'll get to the&nbsp;
bottom of it and then I'll bring them back on set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:28.080
it's these small things can&nbsp;
turn into much bigger ones and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.000
again I want people there who want to be working&nbsp;
and who do feel appreciated and do feel valued and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:41.360
and I also just want a group that loves&nbsp;
making movies as much as I do and and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.720
can can can share in that joy and&nbsp;
that experience and exhilaration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:50.240
and so for me I think it really comes down to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:58.880
being empathetic and also being attuned to other&nbsp;
people and taking them aside for a sort of quick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.560
re-check-in and recalibration if more conversation&nbsp;
needs to be had and then getting right back to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:12.400
why would a director want people around them&nbsp;
that think differently it's so important to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:18.320
collaborators that come from different&nbsp;
walks of life that are into different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:26.640
because ultimately the director's job is to bring&nbsp;
together a bunch of very talented department heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:34.880
actors and to unify a vision going forward&nbsp;
and so if you just have a lot more diversity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:41.680
or a lot more breadth of people's background and&nbsp;
experience to then synthesize into that vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:46.720
it's going to be all the more stronger what&nbsp;
filmmaking rule do you break quite often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:55.520
I think filmmaking rules I break often are maybe&nbsp;
all of them I mean never never at the expense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.000
safety and and union like guidelines obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.840
but I think I'll always that's what philosophy&nbsp;
does is it gets you to sort of question everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

